    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1136">

                <pb facs="1392" n="1378" sameAs="frus:frus1945v09/pg1378"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">833.24/4–1045: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in Uruguay</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Dawson</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-04-14T13:00:00-04:00">April 14, 1945—1
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>164. Department has received a note from the Uruguayan Embassy<anchor n="49"
                        xml:id="d1136-f1"/> urgently requesting remaining deliveries of war
                    materials solicited under Lend Lease. Department has replied to the effect that
                    past requests have been granted to maximum extent compatible with demands of
                    United Nations Armed Forces actively waging war, and with the terms of
                    Lend-Lease Agreement;<anchor n="50" xml:id="d1136-f2"/> that lessened danger of
                    external aggression meant that fewer shipments under Lend-Lease <pb facs="1393"
                        n="1379" xml:id="pg1379"/> could be authorized; that disapproved items of
                    previous requests do not remain pending after final action has been taken on the
                    balance of individual requests; that new requests for respective items must be
                    submitted if additional consideration is desired; and that future Uruguayan
                    requests will be considered in the light of future military requirements and of
                    information obtained from staff conversations.</p>
                <p>Deptel 154, April 7, and urtel 292, April 10.<anchor n="51" xml:id="d1136-f3"/>
                    Department wishes to point out that irrespective of Colombo’s<anchor n="52"
                        xml:id="d1136-f4"/> attitude as of last December allocations of Lend-Lease
                    materials for Uruguayan Army compared most favorably with previous treatment
                    accorded Uruguayan Navy requests.</p>
                <p>Allocations to the Uruguayan Army have so far been in a ratio of more than two to
                    one as compared with the Navy. For your strictly confidential information, the
                    figures as of December 1944 totaled approximately $2,377,000 for the Army
                    (exclusive of the large deliveries which were made available in the last quarter
                    of 1944), as compared with $1,500,000 for the Navy.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stettinius</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
